  <!-- 3. 24-48H DELTA -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The June 17 Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding — produced by Pakistan-Qatar mediation after Switzerland-hosted negotiations — established a 14-point framework: an immediate halt to large-scale US-Iran hostilities, 60 days of toll-free Hormuz transit, termination of the US naval blockade of Iranian ports, and a 60-day clock for a final nuclear settlement. Iran's Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei endorsed the MOU in writing, with stated reservations. The 60-day deadline expired August 17 without a breakthrough. Washington and Tehran remain at odds on the core trio: the Strait of Hormuz, Iran's nuclear program, and sanctions relief. On August 17, Trump publicly demanded Iran's surrender and threatened action against Oman; Tehran announced a shift to a "fully offensive" military posture the same day. Qatar and Pakistan continued message exchanges between the parties post-deadline; Oman-Iran Hormuz-specific talks were described as "close" on August 5 but produced no joint announcement as of August 27. → moved <span class="cite">F1</span> (resolved YES), <span class="cite">F2</span> (resolved YES)</p>

      <p>Mine-countermeasure clearing operations formally commenced April 11 under CENTCOM — USS Frank E. Petersen Jr. and USS Michael Murphy deployed with autonomous survey systems — satisfying <span class="cite">F5</span> well before its July 10 horizon. The post-MOU brief Hormuz opening broke down in early July: IRGC forces struck two tankers on July 31 transiting on "unapproved routes," and Trump stated on July 7 that the pact was "over." US kinetic responses against IRGC assets followed. As of August 26, the strait is effectively closed: three crossings recorded August 23 against a baseline of 85/day. Lloyd's List (August 5) places the late-July average at 12/day; the 50/day threshold required for <span class="cite">F4</span> was never approached. Approximately 341 hulls hold position at approaches; VLCC spot rates are at multi-decade highs. → moved <span class="cite">F4</span> (resolved NO), <span class="cite">F5</span> (resolved YES); supports <span class="cite">N2</span>, <span class="cite">N3</span></p>

      <p>Netanyahu's coalition suffered a parliamentary majority collapse in May 2026 when UTJ (7 seats) and Shas (11 seats) withdrew support after Netanyahu shelved the Haredi draft-exemption bill, reducing the bloc from 68 to approximately 50 seats — below the 61-seat threshold. A dissolution bill's preliminary reading passed 110-0 on May 20. The Knesset officially dispersed on July 17 after a final legislative marathon, fixing the election for October 27. <span class="cite">F6</span> fails on both prongs: the dissolution bill's final passage came July 17 (two days past the July 15 horizon), and the date fixed equals — rather than precedes — October 27. August polling places Likud at 22-24 seats, Eisenkot's Yashar at 23; the coalition bloc at 48 seats (Maariv, August 9), short of a governing majority. The Likud primary (August 17-18) delivered Netanyahu's preferred list, though the bloc's arithmetic remains unfavorable. → moved <span class="cite">F6</span> (resolved NO), <span class="cite">F7</span> (resolved YES); supports <span class="cite">N5</span></p>

      <p>In the June 10-24 window, the April 8 ceasefire remained nominally in force while negotiators worked toward the June 17 MOU. No TIER1 source documents a confirmed bilateral Israel-Iran direct exchange — strikes flowing both directions — within any 48-hour window before June 24. The available record places the first post-ceasefire Israel-Iran direct confrontation after June 24. Post-MOU military activity was predominantly US-Iran rather than bilateral Israel-Iran: IRGC tanker attacks in July triggered US strikes on IRGC assets; Iran fired missiles and drones at Arab states hosting US bases; the US imposed new Hezbollah sanctions on August 20. Trump stated August 17 that the MOU's expiry was not a hard trigger for resumed large-scale land strikes. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span> (resolved NO); supports <span class="cite">N1</span></p>

      <p>Mojtaba Khamenei's last confirmed public appearance was March 8 upon formally taking office; 172 days have elapsed without video or in-person confirmation accepted by TIER1 international outlets. Iranian officials acknowledge injuries from the February 28 strikes without specifying their severity. Iranian state media disclosed in August that images of the new Supreme Leader would be released "soon" — the clearest official acknowledgment that the absence has drawn internal management attention. IRGC commanders directed Hormuz operations autonomously throughout the period. A July 2026 report cited sources in Khamenei's circle expressing openness to nuclear weapons development. IAEA inspectors confirmed facility access under the June MOU; their status and access after August 17 are not publicly confirmed. → <span class="context-only">context only</span>; supports <span class="cite">N4</span>, <span class="cite">N6</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 4. SCENARIO MAP -->
  <div class="section" id="scenario-map">
