      </div>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-esc">Escalation path (23%):</strong> Derived from P(N1 OR N3) = 1&minus;(1&minus;0.12)(1&minus;0.13) = 23%; either a US land strike on Iran or a Brent settlement above $140 would confirm an escalatory trajectory with Hormuz remaining closed through Q4 2026.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-prot">Protracted path (47%):</strong> Neither Oman-Iran framework (N2) nor a US strike (N1) materializes in the near term; the strait remains at low-single-digit daily crossings, back-channel messages persist, and the conflict settles into an attritional standoff with dark-fleet oil exports sustaining both sides.</p>
      <p class="scenario-line"><strong class="label-desc">De-escalation path (30%):</strong> Primary driver N2 (p=38%): an Iran-Oman joint Hormuz framework in the next 11 days would begin restoring commercial transits; secondary driver N6 (p=16%) provides a longer-term diplomatic path if IAEA monitoring and a successor nuclear deal are eventually secured.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- 5. INSIDE IRAN -->
  <div class="section" id="inside-iran">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      Inside Iran
      <span class="meta">State-media decode · 200 words · verb-ban + forecast-cite rule applies</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="inside-iran">
      <div class="iran-media-badges">
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRNA</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">IRIB</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Press TV</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Fars</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Tasnim</span>
        <span class="iran-media-badge">Kayhan</span>
      </div>
      <p>Iran's governing structure since February 28 operates without a confirmed, publicly visible supreme leader. Mojtaba Khamenei formally assumed the leadership on March 8 following his father's assassination, but has not appeared publicly since; Iranian officials acknowledge injuries from the February strikes without specifying their extent. Iranian state media confirmed in August that images of the new Supreme Leader would be released "soon" (globalsecurity.org/RFE/RL, August 9) — an official acknowledgment, recorded August 9, that the absence has drawn internal management attention requiring coordination. → <span class="context-only">context only</span>; supports <span class="cite">N4</span></p>
      <p>The IRGC's Navy and Aerospace Force has directed Hormuz operations autonomously throughout the conflict period, as demonstrated by the July 31 tanker strikes and the "fully offensive" posture declaration on August 17 following the MOU's expiry (RT&#201; News, August 18; Washington Times, July 31). The Assembly of Experts, constitutionally charged with supervising the Supreme Leader, published its last plenary communiqu&#233; in April; no September session is publicly scheduled (Wikipedia, 2026 Iranian leadership crisis). → <span class="context-only">context only</span>; supports <span class="cite">N4</span></p>
      <p>Inside elite circles, a factional division has emerged between IRGC-aligned hardliners around Mojtaba Khamenei's reported preferences — including openness to nuclear weapons development (Newsmax, July 24) — and pragmatists centered on acting Foreign Minister Araghchi, who negotiated the MOU and maintains back-channel contact with Omani and Qatari mediators. Revenue from Chinese and Indian oil purchases via dark tankers sustains IRGC operational budgets, though Hormuz's near-closure restricts Iranian export volumes as well (Lloyd's List Intelligence, August 5). IAEA inspectors confirmed facility access under the June MOU; their presence and formal access rights after August 17 are not publicly confirmed (House of Commons Library, 2026). → <span class="context-only">context only</span>; supports <span class="cite">N4</span>, <span class="cite">N6</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

